
# Transaction Managers
- Transaction Manager is core component in transaction, it is responsible for managing the transactions.
  it is responsible for getting transaction, committing a transaction and rolling back a transaction.
- In Spring Boot, All the transaction operations are handled by transaction manager.
- Transaction Managers is an interface.

- public interface TransactionManager{            //This is just a parent interface, also it is marker interface

}

- public interface PlatformTransactionManager extends TransactionManager{
    public void getTransaction();
    public void commit();
    public void rollback();
}

- now Abstract class AbstractPlatformTransactionManager provides the implementation of 
  getTransaction(), commit(), and rollback() methods.

- now this abstract class is used by different transaction managers(which are concrete classes) such as

- DataSource TransactionManager
- Hibernate TransactionManager
- JPA TransactionManager
- JTA TransactionManager
- JDBC TransactionManager

- So spring will choose one of between concrete classes, most common it choose JPA TransactionManager at runtime.


# Types of TransactionManagers

1) Declarative TransactionManager
2) Programmatic TransactionManager

1) Declarative TransactionManager: 
 - Declarative means declare a annotation and job is done.
 - Transaction Management happens through Annotations
 - @Transactional
 - Spring boot will choose appropriate transaction manager.
 - We can create explicit transaction manager and tell spring to choose that.
Example:
 @Transactional(transactionManager = "transactionManager")

 2) Programmatic TransactionManager
 - Handle Transaction progamatically/manually through code
 - Flexible but difficult to maintain
 - There are two approaches for Programmatic TransactionManager

   i)  Using boilerPlate code by different transaction methods
   ii) Transaction Templates


# Transaction Propagations and Different Types of Transaction Propagations  (IMP)

- Transaction Propagation refers to how a transactions happens when a method annotated with @Transactional is called by another method 
  that may or may not have active transaction.

- Default propagation type is REQUIRED

- There are 7 propagation Types

 1) REQUIRED :  (Joins the exisitng transaction from calling method)
 -  REQUIRED is default propagation type
 -  When propagation type is REQUIRED that means this particualr method requires a transaction.
 -  If transaction is already active it will join the same transaction
 -  And if do not have transaction in calling method then REQUIRED will starts its own transaction
 -  When to use REQUIRED : When we need anything with in the transaction

 2) REQUIRES_NEW
 -  When we want to isolate particualr transaction from other surrounding transactions
 -  REQUIRES_NEW suppends the existing transaction from calling method and starts a new transaction
    Example: Sending a notification, Sending E-mail, logging

 3) SUPPORTS:
 - The particular method (on which @Transactional marked) supports a transaction if the calling method have transaction
 
 4) NOT_SUPPORTED : 
 - If the calling method have existing transaction then transaction for the particular method is suspended and after this method
   the transaction will be resumed again.

 5) MANDATORY :
   - There should be active transaction in calling method. If active transaction is not present in calling method then call method will
     throw an exception that there should be active transaction present.
   - MANDATORY propagation type is useful when we have to enforce that there should not be any new transaction created rather things
     should works inside existing transaction and there must be a existing transaction present while calling a method.

 6) NESTED :
  - Whenever we mark a method with propagation type as NESTED, that means if calling method have the existing transaction then this method
    will run in same transaction in nested way ( and if calling method do not have any transaction then it behaves like default propagation type
    as REQUIRED)
  - The calling method will create a "Save Point" and the calling method will  run ahead of that "Save Point", and if in this method some exception
    occurring then only that particualr transaction after that Save Point will be rollback AND the existing transaction before that save point will continue.
  - NESTED is useful when we have to do partial rollback and we do not want to rollback entire transaction

  7) NEVER:
  - The particular method (on which @Transactional marked) should not run in transaction, if the calling method have transaction then
    the particualr method will throw an exception as already on going transaction

